diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..66a2d75
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,210 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Bots are `User` records with `role: :bot`. They're members of rooms like people, but they
+never sign in. They post through an HTTP API instead. Unlike people, bots do **not** join
+open rooms by default — see [Room membership](#room-membership).
+
+## Model (`User::Bot`)
+
+- `users.bot_token` – random secret (unique index). The **bot code** shown in the UI is
+  `User#bot_key` = `"<id>-<bot_token>"`.
+- `users.creator_id` – the person who created the bot. `User#can_administer?(bot)` already
+  works through `record.creator`; `User#bots` lists a person's bots.
+- `User.new_bot` / `User.create_bot!` set the role, a token and a random password (the
+  `password_digest` column is NOT NULL; bots have no email so they can't log in anyway).
+- `User.authenticate_bot(bot_key)` looks the bot up by id among *active* bots and compares
+  tokens with `secure_compare`.
+- Deleting a bot = `User#deactivate` (keeps its messages, drops memberships) which also
+  clears `bot_token`, so the old code stops working immediately.
+- `User.without_bots` keeps bots out of places that are about people: login, session
+  transfer, account settings user list/role/removal, direct-message placeholders.
+
+## UI (`BotsController`, `/bots`)
+
+- Linked from account settings ("Set up chat bots", `bot.svg`). Every person can use it.
+- Members see the bots they created; administrators see all active bots. Records outside
+  that scope raise `RecordNotFound` (404).
+- Pages send `Cache-Control: no-store` since they show credentials. No pagination.
+- Form params use the `user` key (`form_with model: @bot`).
+
+### Bot limit ("Bots per member")
+
+- `Account#bots_per_member` (`settings.bots_per_member`, `has_json` default `5`, validated as an
+  integer >= 0). Set by administrators in a number field on `/account/edit` that submits on change;
+  invalid values redirect back with an alert instead of raising.
+- `User#reached_bot_limit?` – `false` for administrators; otherwise compares `bots.active.count`
+  (deleted/deactivated bots don't count) with the limit. `User#bot_limit` reads it from `Current.account`.
+- Enforced in the model: `User::Bot` validates `creator_within_bot_limit` **on create**, so every
+  creation path (`BotsController#create`, `User.create_bot!`, consoles) is refused. Being an
+  on-create check, lowering the limit never touches existing bots — it only blocks new ones.
+- UI: at the limit, `bots/index` replaces "Add a chat bot" with a "You've reached the limit…" note,
+  and `BotsController#new` redirects to `/bots` with an alert.
+
+## Room membership
+
+- People join every open room (`User#grant_membership_to_open_rooms`, `unless: :bot?`,
+  and `Rooms::Open#grant_access_to_all_users`). Bots only do when
+  `users.joins_all_open_rooms` is on ("Add to all open rooms" switch in the bot form,
+  off by default). `User.joining_open_rooms` = people + such bots; use it wherever
+  "everyone joins open rooms" logic lives.
+- Turning the switch on (`User::Bot#join_all_open_rooms`, `after_save_commit` on the
+  change to `true`) grants every existing open room; rooms that become open later
+  (created, or converted from closed) pick the bot up in `Rooms::Open`. Turning it off
+  doesn't touch memberships.
+- People add bots by hand: closed rooms through the usual `user_ids[]` switches; open
+  rooms through `bot_ids[]` switches ("Give <bot> access to this room") rendered for bots
+  without the flag in `rooms/opens/_user`. `Rooms::OpensController` revises only bot
+  memberships and only when `bot_ids` is submitted (the form always sends a blank
+  `bot_ids[]`), so a rename-only request never drops bots. Bots with the flag show a
+  plain check (they can't be removed from open rooms while it's on).
+
+### `joined` event
+
+**All** membership creation goes through `room.memberships.grant_to` (also `revise`,
+`Room.create_for`, `Rooms::Open`, `join_all_open_rooms`). It uses
+`insert_all(..., returning: :user_id)`, so only *newly inserted* memberships come back
+(the unique index on `room_id, user_id` drops duplicates) → `Room#deliver_joined_webhooks_later`
+enqueues `Webhook::JoinedDeliveryJob` for those bots with webhooks. That's what makes it
+"exactly once per room" (re-granting, re-submitting forms, toggling the switch off/on don't
+re-announce). A bot that's removed and later re-added is announced again.
+
+- Never for direct rooms (`room.direct?`).
+- Enqueued in `ActiveRecord.after_all_transactions_commit` (the app has
+  `enqueue_after_transaction_commit` off), so a rolled-back grant never announces.
+- `Webhook#deliver_joined` skips the POST if the bot's no longer a member when the job runs.
+- Don't add memberships with a bare `Membership.insert_all` for bots — it bypasses `joined`.
+
+## API (`Messages::ByBotsController`)
+
+`POST /rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages` (`room_bot_messages_path`)
+
+- Raw request body = message body, treated as **plain text** (HTML-escaped, newlines → `<br>`).
+- Multipart requests with an `attachment` file post that file instead.
+- Responses: `201` + `Location: /messages/:id`, `422` (blank body, no attachment),
+  `404` (unknown room or room the bot isn't a member of), `401` (bad/deleted bot code),
+  `429` + `Retry-After` (posting cooldown), `403` (suspended bot) — see [Suspension](#suspension).
+- Auth: `allow_bot_access` (in `Authentication`) skips session auth and CSRF and sets
+  `Current.user` to the bot, so message creation, broadcasts and push notifications are
+  the same code paths as for people.
+
+### Gotchas
+
+- `curl -d` sends `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. Parsing free text as form data
+  breaks (e.g. `100% done` raises a 400), and `Rack::MethodOverride` would parse it before
+  the controller runs. `lib/middleware/bot_message_body.rb` (inserted before
+  `Rack::MethodOverride`) rewrites the content type of non-multipart bot posts to
+  `text/plain`.
+- The bot key is in the URL. `:_key` in `filter_parameters` hides it from the
+  `Parameters:` log line, and `lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_paths.rb` masks it in the
+  `Started POST ...` line. Reverse proxies / access logs outside Rails will still see it.
+
+## Webhooks (`Webhook`, `Webhook::DeliveryJob`)
+
+A bot may have one optional webhook (`webhooks` table, `has_one :webhook` on `User::Bot`,
+unique on `user_id`). The bot form edits it through the virtual `User#webhook_url`
+attribute ("Webhook URL" field): a present URL builds/updates the record, a blank one
+`mark_for_destruction`s it (`autosave: true`), so saving the form with an empty field
+removes the webhook. Only `http(s)://` URLs validate. `User#deactivate` deletes it.
+
+### Trigger
+
+`Message` `after_create_commit` → `Room#receive` → `deliver_webhooks_later`: for each
+**mentioned** bot that is an active room member with a webhook (`User.with_webhooks`,
+`Message#mentionees`), enqueue `Webhook::DeliveryJob`. Never inline: the person posting
+must not wait on the bot. Messages *created by bots* never trigger webhooks, so bots
+replying to (or mentioning) each other can't loop.
+
+Bots already appear in the @-mention autocomplete (`Autocompletable::UsersController`
+doesn't filter by role) — keep it that way.
+
+### Payload (POST, `Content-Type: application/json`)
+
+```json
+{
+  "event":   "mention",
+  "author":  { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room":    { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot_key>/messages" },
+  "message": { "id": 118, "body": { "text": "…", "html": "<div>…</div>" }, "link": "/rooms/5/@118" }
+}
+```
+
+- `event` is `"mention"` here; see `joined` below. Add new event types alongside them
+  (`Webhook#*_payload_for`, sharing `room_payload_for`).
+- `body.text` = `plain_text_body` with the bot's own `@Name` (its
+  `attachable_plain_text_representation`) removed and the result trimmed. Other mentions stay.
+- `body.html` = the stored rich-text HTML (`message.body.body.to_html`).
+- `reply_to` = `room_bot_messages_path(room, bot.bot_key)` (the bot's own posting URL),
+  `link` = `room_at_message_path`. Paths are relative on purpose.
+
+`joined` (bot added to a non-direct room):
+
+```json
+{ "event": "joined", "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot_key>/messages" } }
+```
+
+### Response handling (`Webhook#deliver`, `Webhook#deliver_joined`)
+
+Same for every event (`Webhook#deliver_to`): the reply is posted into the event's room.
+
+- `200` + `text/plain` → escaped like the posting API (newlines → `<br>`) and posted as the bot.
+- `200` + `text/html` → posted as rich text (Action Text sanitizes on render).
+- Anything else (`204`, other content types, errors, bodies > 1 MB) → nothing is posted.
+- 7 s open/read/write timeouts; network errors and timeouts are logged and swallowed.
+  Redirects aren't followed.
+- Replies are created with `creator: bot` explicitly (no `Current.user` in jobs) and
+  broadcast with `broadcast_create`.
+
+### Security note
+
+Webhook delivery does **not** go through `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard`: self-hosted
+bots commonly live on the same private network/localhost as Campfire. The flip side is
+that anyone who can create a bot can make the server POST to internal addresses (and see
+a text/html response posted back). If that matters for a deployment, pin the address
+with `PrivateNetworkGuard.resolve` in `Webhook#post` (as `Opengraph::Fetch` does).
+
+## Suspension (`User::Suspendable`, `Bots::SuspensionsController`)
+
+Misbehaving bots are stopped automatically; only administrators bring them back. All limits
+are constants in code (`User::Suspendable::MESSAGE_LIMIT/MESSAGE_WINDOW/COOLDOWN/ATTEMPTS_TO_SUSPEND`,
+`Webhook::FAILURES_TO_SUSPEND`) — deliberately no settings.
+
+Columns: `users.suspended_at` + `users.suspension_reason` (`"flooding"` / `"failing_webhook"`,
+described by `User::Suspendable::SUSPENSION_REASONS`), `users.posting_cooldown_ends_at` +
+`users.posting_cooldown_attempts`, `webhooks.consecutive_failures`.
+
+A suspended bot is **still `status: active`** on purpose: that's what keeps its memberships,
+messages, place in `bots.active.count` (bot limit) and listing on `/bots`. Don't model suspension
+as a new `status` value. Places that must ignore suspended bots check `suspended?` /
+`not_suspended` explicitly.
+
+### Flooding
+
+- Every message a bot posts goes through `User#post_within_rate_limit { ... }` — the API
+  (`Messages::ByBotsController#create`) **and** webhook replies (`Webhook#reply_to`). Add any
+  new bot posting path there too.
+- The window is counted from the bot's own `messages` (`created_at` in the last 10 s, sliding),
+  under `with_lock` on the bot row, so concurrent posts can't overshoot. Rejected posts aren't
+  messages, so they never count.
+- The 11th message isn't created and sets a 30 s cooldown. Every post attempt during the
+  cooldown (API or webhook reply) increments `posting_cooldown_attempts`; the 5th suspends the
+  bot (that request still answers 429; the next one gets 403).
+- `Throttled`/`Suspended` are raised **after** the lock's transaction commits (the method sets a
+  flag inside), otherwise raising would roll back the cooldown bookkeeping.
+- The controller creates the message inside the lock but calls `process_attachment` after:
+  Active Storage uploads on commit, so processing inside the transaction would fail.
+- Webhook replies during a cooldown are dropped (logged), not queued.
+
+### Failing webhooks
+
+- `Webhook#deliver_to` counts a failure for any non-2xx response (redirects included), timeout
+  or connection/SSL error (SQL increment, so concurrent jobs don't lose counts); 10 in a row
+  suspend with `failing_webhook`. Any 2xx resets the count. Throttled replies aren't failures.
+- Suspended bots get no webhooks: `User.with_webhooks` excludes them (no jobs enqueued) and
+  `deliver_to` skips jobs that were enqueued before the suspension.
+
+### UI and reactivation
+
+- `bots/_bot` shows "Suspended" plus the reason for suspended bots (owners see it too).
+- Administrators get a "Reactivate" button (`DELETE /bots/:bot_id/suspension`, native confirm via
+  `data-turbo-confirm`). `Bots::SuspensionsController` uses `ensure_can_administer` (403 for
+  members). `User#reactivate` clears the suspension, the cooldown and the webhook failure count.
diff --git a/lib/middleware/bot_message_body.rb b/lib/middleware/bot_message_body.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..53c388c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/middleware/bot_message_body.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Bots post messages with the raw request body, typically via `curl -d "..."`, which
+# labels the body as form data. Parsing free text as form data mangles it or fails
+# outright (e.g. "100% done" isn't valid %-encoding), so requests to the bot messages
+# endpoint are treated as plain text unless they're multipart uploads.
+class BotMessageBody
+  PATH = %r{\A/rooms/[^/]+/[^/]+/messages(\.\w+)?\z}
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if bot_message?(env) && !multipart?(env)
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+
+  private
+    def bot_message?(env)
+      env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && env["PATH_INFO"].to_s.match?(PATH)
+    end
+
+    def multipart?(env)
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"].to_s.start_with?("multipart/form-data")
+    end
+end
diff --git a/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_paths.rb b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_paths.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7abddb1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/rails_ext/filtered_bot_key_paths.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# Bot keys are credentials embedded in the bot messages URL (/rooms/:room_id/:bot_key/messages).
+# Parameter filtering hides them from the "Parameters:" log line, but Rails also logs the
+# request path ("Started POST ..."), so mask the key there too.
+module FilteredBotKeyPaths
+  BOT_MESSAGES_PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/?]+(/messages(?:\.\w+)?)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def filtered_path
+    @filtered_bot_key_path ||= super.sub(BOT_MESSAGES_PATH, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+end
+
+ActiveSupport.on_load(:action_dispatch_request) { prepend FilteredBotKeyPaths }
